  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Added a generated FLINT FmpzMatrix-to-minimal-width byte-region reduction and routed dense ZZ Matrix.change_ring(GF(p)) through it without scalar entry calls, serialization, IntegerBuffer decoding, or host Integer lists. GF(2) enters the existing canonical M4RI resource directly from packed bytes when available; other supported small primes enter canonical UInt64 storage. Public and raw-boundary tests cover huge signed skew entries, empty shapes, closed resources, malformed modulus/width inputs, mutation snapshots, and native-disabled execution. The SageMath 10.9 oracle agrees for p=2,3,97,65521,4294967291. On one million entries, warm conversion measured 6.5 ms for GF(2) and 19.3 ms for GF(97), versus 87.4 ms through host Integer materialization.",
    "risks": [
      "FmpzMatrix resources remain declared wasm=False, so this boundary inherits the existing native-only exact-integer resource capability rather than proving a new browser lowering.",
      "The generated copied-byte ownership contract performs one FLINT-to-host copy; GF(2) then performs the existing host-to-M4RI copied ingress because independently generated libraries cannot share raw pointers. The measured million-entry path remains 6.5 ms."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Integrate the commit on dense-qq-resources, add test/fmpz-matrix-modp-conversion.cjs to test/node-test-manifest.cjs in the manifest-owning integration lane, and retain the benchmark as a regression/crossover witness.",
      "Consider a general generated cross-library byte transfer only if profiling finds the GF(2) copy material; do not add shared raw-pointer ownership speculatively."
    ]
  }
